WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:07.350
Over one year, ago I quit my job as a pentester
to live of bug bounty and creating content,

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:12.750
even though before that, I only had one bounty
to my name.

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:15.879
So by no means, it was a safe bet.

00:00:15.879 --> 00:00:21.730
But I thought that I was finding bugs every
single week during pentests so I expected

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:25.840
that it bug bounty, I can also find that many
bugs.

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:32.540
I imagined that after one year, I would have
some 5-digit bounties to my name and maybe

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:38.050
a 6-digit sum.

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:48.879
Unfortunately, it's not how it went...

00:00:48.879 --> 00:00:55.080
But in this video, I'm showing you exact numbers
of how many reports I submitted and how much

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:56.539
money I made.

00:00:56.539 --> 00:00:57.539
Why?

00:00:57.539 --> 00:01:00.579
Why is the transparency so important to me?

00:01:00.579 --> 00:01:07.091
Because I know that a lot of you are just
like me when I was first hearing about bug bounty.

00:01:07.490 --> 00:01:12.780
Seeing a lot of bragging on Twitter which
most of the time we can't even verify.

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:19.710
Seeing many write-ups that seem simple, seem
like nothing extraordinary yet they are awarded

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:22.280
thousands of dollars of bounties.

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:28.570
This leads to unrealistic expectations and
I want to do my best to help as many of you

00:01:28.570 --> 00:01:37.623
out there as possible by simply being transparent
myself, not only when it's time to celebrate.

00:01:40.899 --> 00:01:47.790
I started off by challenging myself to spend
100 hours on a public program on Hackerone.

00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:49.710
I chose Stripe.

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:56.440
I didn't do any recon - my methodology was
to understand how stuff works and this led

00:01:56.440 --> 00:02:04.149
me to finding two XSSes, an SSRF, an auth bypass
or even being able to purchase my own BBRE

00:02:04.149 --> 00:02:07.960
Premium subscription at a lower price.

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:12.310
I earned $7,200 which was a very good start.

00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:17.700
It wasn't a life-changing money but to be
honest, I didn't think this methodology would

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:19.470
work at all.

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:24.950
Especially, that this was just a public bug
bounty program on the most popular platform

00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:26.240
out there.

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:30.650
This just showed me that I can do bug bounty
for a living.

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:35.890
Especially, that it was the beginning and
I only expected it to get better with the

00:02:35.890 --> 00:02:39.390
future projects.

00:02:39.390 --> 00:02:42.250
But from there, it went worse.

00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:48.970
The next challenge was Elastic bug bounty
program where I also wanted to spend 100 hours.

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:59.200
However, I only found one bug for which I
got $584. 100 hours for me means a full month

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:06.360
if I only did that so this amount of money
is less than I would earn by going to any

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:08.569
job with a minimum wage.

00:03:08.569 --> 00:03:16.599
I did regret publicly obliging myself to spend
the time because I didn't really enjoy it.

00:03:16.599 --> 00:03:20.110
t led to a lot of procrastination.

00:03:20.110 --> 00:03:27.659
But integrity has always been very important
for me so I did complete the 100 hours.

00:03:27.659 --> 00:03:32.650
This challenge made me question my methodology
of not doing any recon.

00:03:32.650 --> 00:03:38.129
I also started hacking on some private programs
that I knew nothing about and I didn't care

00:03:38.129 --> 00:03:39.129
about.

00:03:39.129 --> 00:03:43.849
I also started doing recon even though I do
not like doing recon.

00:03:43.849 --> 00:03:49.220
And most of the time, nothing of this worked.

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:54.530
In that period, there was only one private
program with an application that I use daily

00:03:54.530 --> 00:03:58.080
and I care about and I really enjoyed hacking
there.

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:06.079
In 37 hours, I found four bugs and made $2,500
but most importantly, it was a really, really

00:04:06.079 --> 00:04:08.189
good time for me.

00:04:08.189 --> 00:04:14.989
But after that, I struggled to find another
one that I would really like.

00:04:14.989 --> 00:04:21.199
I'm not even going to count how many of them
there were.

00:04:21.199 --> 00:04:26.250
And just like that, I realized that a year
has passed by.

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:31.919
I thought that after a year, I would have
much more confidence and much more bugs.

00:04:31.919 --> 00:04:37.330
But to be honest, it's not the return on investment
that was my biggest problem.

00:04:37.330 --> 00:04:43.199
I saw that pretty much every program that
I dedicated more time into, I was finding

00:04:43.199 --> 00:04:44.900
bugs.

00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:49.260
I mean everyone apart from Elastic.

00:04:49.260 --> 00:04:52.940
But it was the time spent that was the biggest
problem.

00:04:52.940 --> 00:04:58.500
Often, I was procrastinating when I was just
trying to complete hours for the challenge

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:06.560
or I just couldn't find a target that I would
enjoy hacking daily and I would be motivated.

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:12.530
Some of it is justified because I am not a
full-time bug bounty hunter.

00:05:12.530 --> 00:05:17.910
The other half of my business also takes a
lot of time: creating videos, writing the

00:05:17.910 --> 00:05:21.380
newsletter but also BBRE Premium.

00:05:21.380 --> 00:05:27.479
There, people pay me to learn web security
so it's a completely different thing.

00:05:27.479 --> 00:05:33.960
In this first year, I had to familiarize myself
with things like marketing, creating a website,

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:39.590
taking payments and many, many more that you
don't normally see.

00:05:39.590 --> 00:05:46.389
My plan was to spend roughly 50% of the time
on bug bounty and 50% on the other stuff but

00:05:46.389 --> 00:05:51.770
in reality, the balance was shifted much more
towards the other stuff.

00:05:51.770 --> 00:05:56.000
But to be honest, the lack of time wasn't
the issue here.

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:57.000
I had the time.

00:05:57.000 --> 00:05:59.830
I just didn't use it to work.

00:05:59.830 --> 00:06:06.229
And I have a love-hate relationship with personal
growth but I consider myself an organized

00:06:06.229 --> 00:06:12.229
person and I thought that even without having
a boss, I would not have issues with time

00:06:12.229 --> 00:06:14.090
management.

00:06:14.090 --> 00:06:20.360
But the truth is that over the months, I got
less and less disciplined and simply said,

00:06:20.360 --> 00:06:21.360
lazy.

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:25.560
I started spending less time working overall.

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:31.750
And make no mistake, it was one of my goals
when quitting my job.

00:06:31.750 --> 00:06:36.259
I was tired of working part-time and studying
daily.

00:06:36.259 --> 00:06:39.930
Then working full-time and creating content
after hours.

00:06:39.930 --> 00:06:44.000
And there were huge positives in my life after
quitting.

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:47.500
I finally had the time to meet with my friends.

00:06:47.500 --> 00:06:51.500
I finally had the time to travel.

00:06:51.500 --> 00:06:56.310
I could spend more time on hobbies and sports.

00:06:56.310 --> 00:07:02.205
This year I found my new passion of bouldering
which I absolutely love.

00:07:03.007 --> 00:07:06.861
I also got into the best shape of my life.

00:07:12.470 --> 00:07:19.259
All these things are awesome and I'm so privileged
to live a life like that but I feel in the

00:07:19.259 --> 00:07:23.470
long term, it's the work that gives me the
satisfaction.

00:07:23.470 --> 00:07:26.710
It's still something that I need to be happy.

00:07:26.710 --> 00:07:31.870
But this summer, I hit the low point of motivation.

00:07:31.870 --> 00:07:37.139
Some personal issues at the same time didn't
help either.

00:07:37.139 --> 00:07:43.199
I realized that if I didn't improve, I would
have to change something.

00:07:43.199 --> 00:07:48.840
Maybe even come back to employment.

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:51.409
So things had to change.

00:07:51.409 --> 00:07:55.200
The initial surge of motivation came from
outside.

00:07:55.200 --> 00:08:00.759
When I was in Budapest in the beginning of
August, I texted David Shütz, the author of

00:08:00.759 --> 00:08:05.650
a few bugs covered on my channel and the first
guest of my podcast.

00:08:05.650 --> 00:08:09.000
He's from Hungary so I thought it would be
cool to meet with him.

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:15.350
I ended up staying in Budapest two more days
and we rented an Airbnb to hack something

00:08:15.350 --> 00:08:17.490
together.

00:08:17.490 --> 00:08:22.800
We decided to hack Facebook and it was my
first conventional collab.

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:28.889
But to be honest, I didn't believe that we
can find a bug in Facebook in one day.

00:08:28.889 --> 00:08:34.860
I was still happy to just hack with David,
spend time with him and simply learn.

00:08:34.860 --> 00:08:44.519
But to my surprise, we did find a bug for
which Facebook later paid us $5,000.

00:08:44.519 --> 00:08:51.320
I was really happy about it, especially that
Facebook is just one of these companies that

00:08:51.320 --> 00:08:55.200
you want to hack at some point of your career.

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:56.960
And I came home motivated.

00:08:56.960 --> 00:09:02.899
I started to make some changes in my life
to get my productivity back.

00:09:02.899 --> 00:09:07.320
I started with small and simple things like
keeping my apartment clean.

00:09:07.320 --> 00:09:10.850
I think it's a really good way to create the
discipline.

00:09:10.850 --> 00:09:18.430
I also decided to make bug bounty my priority
over creating content which, of course, resulted

00:09:18.430 --> 00:09:19.540
in fewer videos.

00:09:19.540 --> 00:09:25.670
I will probably never find the equilibrium
but I will always be looking for the balance.

00:09:25.670 --> 00:09:31.550
I also removed some apps from my phone to
decrease my screen time.

00:09:31.550 --> 00:09:36.779
And I started to wake up at 5:30.

00:09:36.779 --> 00:09:41.800
I just love working in the morning and it
also really helped.

00:09:41.800 --> 00:09:47.779
Most importantly, I stopped trying to hack
the way I think other people hack but I started

00:09:47.779 --> 00:09:53.769
focusing on what I like to be doing which
is getting deep into the application, preferably

00:09:53.769 --> 00:09:55.570
an open-source target.

00:09:55.570 --> 00:10:00.490
I wanted to hack something that I use myself
and I care about.

00:10:00.490 --> 00:10:01.490
I chose Todoist.

00:10:01.490 --> 00:10:06.900
The app I use for checklists, even though
their self-hosted bug bounty program only

00:10:06.900 --> 00:10:10.240
pays up to $1,000.

00:10:10.240 --> 00:10:15.480
After only two hours on the program, I found
an Oauth account takeover which further boosted

00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:16.480
my motivation.

00:10:16.480 --> 00:10:22.790
It was a really nice sequence because only
two weeks before, I created a video about

00:10:22.790 --> 00:10:28.450
an Oauth account takeover and a week before,
I created an article in BBRE Premium about

00:10:28.450 --> 00:10:30.990
it and then, I found one myself.

00:10:30.990 --> 00:10:38.300
Later, turned out that the experience with
this self-hosted program was absolutely horrendous.

00:10:38.300 --> 00:10:44.149
They took months to process the bug and then,
they wanted to pay for it as a medium because

00:10:44.149 --> 00:10:45.770
it's a CSRF.

00:10:45.770 --> 00:10:48.050
I'm still arguing about it.

00:10:48.050 --> 00:10:55.510
But then, I didn't know about it and I was
just motivated because of finding such a serious vulnerability.

00:10:56.510 --> 00:11:01.559
Then, I continued to Stripe and I found a
$2,000 bug.

00:11:01.559 --> 00:11:05.040
The write-up of this is already published
on my channel.

00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:10.180
Next, I went to what I love the most - the
open-source target.

00:11:10.180 --> 00:11:15.700
I chose Discourse and in a few days, I found
3 bugs.

00:11:15.700 --> 00:11:21.750
One of them was a duplicate from about a year
ago, the other one is still being triaged

00:11:21.750 --> 00:11:26.089
but one is already fixed and I got $1,024
for it.

00:11:26.089 --> 00:11:29.800
Did I change anything significant in my methodology?

00:11:29.800 --> 00:11:31.579
No I didn't.

00:11:31.579 --> 00:11:37.980
I still only wanted to understand the app
using Burp, by reading the code and by thinking

00:11:37.980 --> 00:11:42.100
about what can I mess up here and there.

00:11:42.100 --> 00:11:48.050
Those bugs weren't the result of a single
magical tool or a single magical trick.

00:11:48.050 --> 00:11:54.670
It was a result of me continuously learning,
evolving, making mistakes and getting experienced.

00:11:54.670 --> 00:11:57.829
I describe things I learn in BBRE Premium.

00:11:57.829 --> 00:12:02.660
If you're interested in joining, on Black
Friday there will be a promotion.

00:12:02.660 --> 00:12:03.900
One of very few I make.

00:12:03.900 --> 00:12:08.350
To not miss that, join my mailing list.

00:12:08.350 --> 00:12:13.829
But coming back to the methodology, I think
this should be one of the most important takeaways

00:12:13.829 --> 00:12:15.050
from this video.

00:12:15.050 --> 00:12:21.350
Do not try to force yourself into doing something
you don't like, into using other people's

00:12:21.350 --> 00:12:22.910
methodologies.

00:12:22.910 --> 00:12:25.889
Bug bounty is not a pentest - you don't have
to do everything.

00:12:25.889 --> 00:12:31.000
Here, we have a lot of freedom and you can
choose your hacking style.

00:12:31.000 --> 00:12:37.290
You can adjust your hacking style to what
you like to do and do what you're good at.

00:12:37.290 --> 00:12:44.079
Now, I'm telling you bounties for all these
bugs but, of course, at that time, they took

00:12:44.079 --> 00:12:49.329
weeks or months to be processed so I didn't
know the responses straight away.

00:12:49.329 --> 00:12:53.850
When I was waiting for those decisions, I
decided to switch things up.

00:12:53.850 --> 00:13:00.380
I think the period after reporting a series
of bugs is the best time to try out something

00:13:00.380 --> 00:13:02.350
new and learn new stuff.

00:13:02.350 --> 00:13:07.149
Not the time when you're not finding bugs
and you feel like you should have changed

00:13:07.149 --> 00:13:12.120
your methodology yesterday but the time after
you have some payouts coming, you know you

00:13:12.120 --> 00:13:13.130
did a good job.

00:13:13.130 --> 00:13:19.020
Then, you can dedicate some time into learning
something new and even if it doesn't work,

00:13:19.020 --> 00:13:24.089
even if you don't monetize this period, nothing
bad will happen.

00:13:24.089 --> 00:13:29.839
I decided that I will dedicate time to submit
my first CodeQL query.

00:13:29.839 --> 00:13:34.709
It's something that I spoke about for a long
time and I covered it in multiple articles

00:13:34.709 --> 00:13:37.680
in BBRE newsletter.

00:13:37.680 --> 00:13:43.670
It's not like any other bug bounty program
and it's quite complex so I thought it would

00:13:43.670 --> 00:13:49.370
take me a long time before I can finally submit
a new query.

00:13:49.370 --> 00:13:58.110
But within 16 hours, I found a CVE that wasn't
detected by CodeQL scanner so I made an improvement

00:13:58.110 --> 00:14:02.600
to the code so similar bugs are not missed
in the future.

00:14:02.600 --> 00:14:08.769
My pull request was accepted so my code is
already running in CodeQL.

00:14:08.769 --> 00:14:16.720
However, I am still waiting for CVE numbers
which I need to be eligible for a bounty.

00:14:16.720 --> 00:14:21.519
But most importantly, I'm really happy to
check this off my list.

00:14:21.519 --> 00:14:27.019
I know I'm speaking about a lot of bugs that
are not disclosed here so let me know in the

00:14:27.019 --> 00:14:31.050
comment section down below which bugs should
I cover first.

00:14:31.050 --> 00:14:34.649
Which ones are you most interested in?

00:14:34.649 --> 00:14:39.960
After CodeQL, I came back to more regular
hunting and I chose Google.

00:14:39.960 --> 00:14:43.720
Another company that I wanted to hack for
a long time.

00:14:43.720 --> 00:14:49.899
I got really lucky because in two hours I
found a bug for which Google later paid me

00:14:49.899 --> 00:14:51.230
$3,133.7.

00:14:51.230 --> 00:14:58.139
Later, I found another one in the same asset
and very similar bug but more impactful.

00:14:58.139 --> 00:15:03.709
It's still triaged and I don't know how it
will go but to be honest, I expect it to be

00:15:03.709 --> 00:15:07.269
my highest bounty.

00:15:07.269 --> 00:15:14.949
The sum of bounties for this period is $19,501.7
with potentially pending payouts from Todoist

00:15:14.949 --> 00:15:17.060
CodeQL and Google.

00:15:17.060 --> 00:15:22.170
The time I logged for those bugs is 441 hours.

00:15:22.170 --> 00:15:28.019
To be honest, I think I could make more by
being employed or by focusing on freelance

00:15:28.019 --> 00:15:33.399
pentests which I may actually do in Q4 when
everyone is busy.

00:15:33.399 --> 00:15:36.000
But it's not how I look at it.

00:15:36.000 --> 00:15:42.899
I know that one year ago, odds weren't on
my side and now I see that I am improving

00:15:42.899 --> 00:15:46.560
and I am finding bugs whenever I put the time
in.

00:15:46.560 --> 00:15:52.370
I am better than I was one year ago and this
is the benchmark for me and not my Twitter

00:15:52.370 --> 00:15:53.370
feed.

00:15:53.370 --> 00:16:01.090
I know that when people make earning bounties
look easy, probably they just spent a lot

00:16:01.090 --> 00:16:05.519
of time working hard before.

00:16:05.519 --> 00:16:11.630
I'm just happy I can continue living my life
this way - being my own boss and creating

00:16:11.630 --> 00:16:13.660
my own rules.

00:16:13.660 --> 00:16:20.040
I won't buy a home but I have to rent one
or I won't buy a sports car but if these are

00:16:20.040 --> 00:16:25.430
my biggest life problems at 24, then I take it.

00:16:25.430 --> 00:16:31.953
Next year I'm going to try the digital nomad
lifestyle so maybe I won't need these things anyway

00:16:33.250 --> 00:16:37.510
So whatever you want to achieve in life, be
disciplined.

00:16:37.510 --> 00:16:41.610
Putting in the time consistently is the most
important thing.

00:16:41.610 --> 00:16:47.459
Don't be afraid to fail, don't compare yourself
to others, don't be afraid to make mistakes

00:16:47.459 --> 00:16:53.287
and these things will give you the courage
to make the risky decision and then make it work.

